      "preview_head": "e was active in Athens between c. 480 and 460 BC. Many vases have been attributed to his hand on the basis of style. John Beazley gave him the name from a skyphos now at Schwerin with a signature indi",
      "preview_tail": " Classical style\". Some of his best pieces, however, were produced in the White Ground Technique. The most important motifs of his paintings are horses, warriors, and scenes from symposia. His work is",
      "full_text": "e was active in Athens between c. 480 and 460 BC. Many vases have been attributed to his hand on the basis of style. John Beazley gave him the name from a skyphos now at Schwerin with a signature indicating that it was made by the potter Pistoxenos. It depicts Iphikles being taught music by Linos, and Heracles accompanied by his tattooed Thracian servant Geropso. The Pistoxenos Painter probably started his apprenticeship under the Antiphon Painter in the workshop of Euphronios. He specialized in kylikes, which he painted in the red-figure style, bringing \"the delicacy of Late Archaic vase-painting into the Early Classical style\". Some of his best pieces, however, were produced in the White Ground Technique. The most important motifs of his paintings are horses, warriors, and scenes from symposia. His work is",
